WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.468
- Early on,

00:00:03.737 --> 00:00:06.406
as the clinical trials
were still progressing

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:08.541 align:start size:81% position:19%
and then at the
very earliest time

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:09.743
when they started
deploying this,

00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:11.111
it became clear that

00:00:12.746 --> 00:00:14.180
one of the adverse,

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.018 align:start size:97% position:3%
there was two in particular
that were popping up,

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:20.553
that my buddies at the FDA,

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:23.256
who were outside of
the review branch

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:25.925
that I was having
weekly zooms with,

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:29.195
said were being flagged.

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:31.965 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I was hearing it popping
up from other physicians.

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:33.767
One was

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:36.136
myocarditis,

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:40.240
which the FDA and
the CDC denied,

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:42.809
and the Israelis denied.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:43.810
That's a story.

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:50.050 align:start size:97% position:3%
And how that, how that
finally came, became public.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:55.021
And the other one
was reactivation of
latent DNA viruses,

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.858
shingles being a
notable example.

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:02.896
And so there's a number of these

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:06.132
large herpes like DNA viruses

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:08.735 align:start size:84% position:16%
that exist in our
bodies for most of us.

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:11.504
Cytomegalovirus is another one.

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:14.074 align:start size:81% position:19%
Epstein-Barr virus
is a major one.

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:18.611 align:start size:88% position:13%
And generally your
T-cells keep these guys,

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:22.015 align:start position:0%
you can think of it as Pandora's
box, keeps the lid shut

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:25.819 align:start size:84% position:16%
by, you know,
T-cell suppression.

00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:27.253
And if you,

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:30.557 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you pull that suppression
off, the top pops open

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:33.693
and all the nasties fly
out of Pandora's box,

00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:35.095
I think is a good
way to think about

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:37.497
these latent DNA viruses.

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:40.467
And it was clear that that
was happening after the jab.

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:43.036
(upbeat music)

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.975 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm Dave Rubin
and joining me today

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:51.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
is a global clinical
research scholar

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:54.147
at Harvard Medical
School, a scientist,

00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:57.250 align:start size:84% position:16%
an author, a speaker,
a bioethicist,

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.920 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the inventor of
mRNA vaccine technology,

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.122
Dr. Robert Malone.

00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:03.823
Welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:06.126
- Thank you so much.

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:09.429
It's lovely to see you here
today on this rather cold

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.798
Saturday here in Virginia.

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:15.668 align:start size:81% position:19%
But it's sunny and
it's, you know,

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:18.171 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's looking like it's gonna
be a good year, I think.

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:19.405
- And you mentioned
to me that you were

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:20.507
just out on the farm.

00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:22.342
So you're dressed down a bit

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:25.478
for your average
podcast appearance.

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:27.914
- Yeah, I had, I'm sorry.

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:29.883
This didn't get on
the schedule somehow

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:33.253 align:start position:0%
and I've got a bunch of hay
and straw that I have to stack.

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:36.256
We have a young foal in the barn

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:40.326
that's a little sketchy and
it's gonna be a cold night,

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:43.163
so we wanna make sure
he's well bedded down.

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:45.031 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Nice, well, then
we'll use your time well

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:47.467
so that you can get
to that, you know.

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:50.803 align:start size:97% position:3%
So when I was doing some
research for this interview,

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:54.507 align:start size:84% position:16%
your bio is basically
pages and pages.

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.743
I mean, we just picked
some highlights there,

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:59.312 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I was trying to remember
some of the chronology

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:00.547
of these last couple years,

00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:01.948
which have gotten very weird

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:03.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
in light of COVID.
- Yeah, to say the least.

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:05.952
- But I'm actually, I'm to say
the least, to say the least.

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:08.454 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm actually not sure-
- I would say surreal.

00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:11.090
- Surreal, yeah, exactly.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.926
We are, we are in
the matrix now,

00:03:12.926 --> 00:03:17.297 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I'm actually not sure
if I had heard of you before

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:21.534
that famous or infamous
appearance on Joe Rogan.

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:24.070 align:start size:84% position:16%
So maybe before we
get to what's happened

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:25.505
for the last couple years,

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:27.574
do you wanna just give
us a little bit of recap

00:03:27.574 --> 00:03:30.243
of your career before that
that led to this moment?

00:03:30.243 --> 00:03:31.644 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then that's really
where I wanna spend

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:33.179
most of the interview.

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:36.616
- Well, I'm, I don't want
to belabor this too much.

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:38.785
I'm a physician and a scientist

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:42.555
trained at UC San Diego,
Salk Institute, UC Davis,

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:45.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Northwestern
University Medical School

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:47.660 align:start size:84% position:16%
and Harvard University
Medical School.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.395
So that's the high level.

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:57.370 align:start size:97% position:3%
I been passionate about
molecular biology and virology

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.172
pretty much my whole career.

00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:03.476
And also virology, with
the vaccine emphasis,

00:04:03.476 --> 00:04:08.047 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is kind of what's
brought me into the current.

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:12.051
And my focus back when I
was a graduate student,

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:14.287 align:start size:88% position:13%
was trying to understand
some fundamentals

00:04:14.287 --> 00:04:16.789
about RNA structure function,

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:21.127
and particularly how RNA gets
inserted into a retrovirus.

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:23.463 align:start size:84% position:16%
And in order to ask
fundamental questions,

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:28.468
I had to design ways to
make large quantities

00:04:29.636 --> 00:04:31.304
of purified RNA and
put it into cells

00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:34.507
and detect its presence
and blah, blah, blah.

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:37.644
And it just happened, I
was in a gene therapy lab

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:40.246
working on gene therapy projects

00:04:40.246 --> 00:04:43.850 align:start size:84% position:16%
when I had this
series of discoveries

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.819
that made me aware that
there was a potential

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:48.321
to use RNA as a drug

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:53.593 align:start position:0%
and of the various
applications for a gene therapy

00:04:53.593 --> 00:04:54.761
or gene delivery system

00:04:54.761 --> 00:04:56.729 align:start size:84% position:16%
that doesn't actually
work very well.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.765
That being with the
cationic lipids.

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:03.870
But the kind of entry-level
application was vaccines.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.571
And so that was the
origin of the DNA

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:07.073
and the RNA vaccine idea.

00:05:08.074 --> 00:05:10.610
Fast forward through, you know,

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.445
a decade and a half of academia

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:15.081
at UC Davis School of Pathology,

00:05:15.081 --> 00:05:17.650
U Maryland School of Pathology,

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.586
both teaching medical schools-

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:23.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
Medical students,
I'm sorry, pathology,

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:25.825 align:start size:81% position:19%
and then Armed
Forces University,

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:27.327
the health sciences,

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:30.029 align:start size:97% position:3%
where I helped set up the
clinical breast care program

00:05:30.029 --> 00:05:33.933
in a research institute
in Windber, Pennsylvania.

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:38.938 align:start size:88% position:13%
I then started a
company called a INOVIO,

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:40.907
that's still running,

00:05:40.907 --> 00:05:43.209
that was largely based
on my own technology

00:05:43.209 --> 00:05:45.645
and that of others in
the field at the time.

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:49.682 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then the planes
hit the towers,

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:53.353
the investors for
INOVIO pulled back.

00:05:53.353 --> 00:05:57.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
My wife and I were left
high and dry in Rockville.

00:05:57.357 --> 00:05:59.258
And I reached out to a buddy

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:03.529 align:start size:97% position:3%
at the Business and
Economic Development office

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:06.866
at Maryland State, and he said,

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:10.303 align:start size:97% position:3%
hey, there's this new company
up in Frederick, Maryland

00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:12.905 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I could probably
make a phone call

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:14.273
and you could get a job at.

00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:17.043
That was DynPort
Vaccine Company,

00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:20.813
which had just received,
what the technical term is,

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:24.817
the prime systems contract
for all bio-defense products

00:06:24.817 --> 00:06:27.019 align:start size:84% position:16%
for the US Department
of Defense.

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:30.456 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so I became their
associate clinical director

00:06:30.456 --> 00:06:34.427 align:start size:94% position:6%
and basically was involved
in clinical research

00:06:34.427 --> 00:06:38.831
on virtually all
drugs, vaccines,

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:40.466
and monoclonal antibody products

00:06:40.466 --> 00:06:43.603 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the DOD was
developing for bio-defense.

00:06:43.603 --> 00:06:46.672
And that was really the
pivot point in my career

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:49.976
where I shifted from being
a discovery research guy

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:53.813
and finally had the realization

00:06:53.813 --> 00:06:57.583 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the world really
doesn't need more

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:58.885
academic thought leaders.

00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:00.453
It has plenty of those.

00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:05.425 align:start size:97% position:3%
But what it does need is
people who can bridge the gap.

00:07:06.793 --> 00:07:08.294 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's actually called the
Valley of Death, technically.

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:13.065
A term Phil Russell employed
between the discovery part

00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:15.435
in actually making a product.

00:07:15.435 --> 00:07:18.271
And I decided that I
had spent enough time,

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:23.476 align:start size:88% position:13%
various euphemisms come
to mind, let's say,

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:25.011
chasing academia

00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:31.083
and decided that I
wanted to actually

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:33.920 align:start size:94% position:6%
try to make things that
made people's lives better

00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:36.289
and resulted in products.

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:41.093
So I shifted gears
and really focused on

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:45.131
learning the trade of
clinical development,

00:07:46.365 --> 00:07:48.801
regulatory affairs,
project management,

00:07:48.801 --> 00:07:50.102
proposal development,

00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:53.473 align:start size:81% position:19%
that whole toolkit
that goes into

00:07:53.473 --> 00:07:58.478
being able to assemble teams
to solve complicated problems

00:07:59.912 --> 00:08:01.747
and get them funded

00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:05.117
in the space of the government,

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:07.520 align:start size:84% position:16%
in particularly
Department of Defense.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:10.623
And I've, I've been
study section chair

00:08:10.623 --> 00:08:15.628
on many large contract
bid selection processes.

00:08:17.096 --> 00:08:21.367
I've served the government
in writing solicitations.

00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:26.239
And then eventually I set up
my own consulting practice,

00:08:26.239 --> 00:08:29.308 align:start size:97% position:3%
kind of got tired of working
for corporate America.

00:08:29.308 --> 00:08:33.746 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'd worked for a Bill and
Melinda Gates funded company

00:08:33.746 --> 00:08:36.782
called ArisGlobal TV
Vaccine Foundation,

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:38.918
also Path in Seattle.

00:08:40.086 --> 00:08:44.090
Worked for Salve on as
their clinical director

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:46.859
for influenza vaccines.

00:08:46.859 --> 00:08:48.928
You know, many of these kind
of things I just got tired of,

00:08:48.928 --> 00:08:52.031
of working for corporate
and having to toe the line

00:08:52.031 --> 00:08:53.766
and do the eight to five.

00:08:53.766 --> 00:08:57.537 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I set up a
consulting practice

00:08:57.537 --> 00:09:00.339
and that's kind of what
I've done ever since.

00:09:00.339 --> 00:09:05.311 align:start size:97% position:3%
But given my background, I'm
known as a specialist in,

00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:07.113
like I said, assembling teams

00:09:07.113 --> 00:09:09.348
to solve complicated problems

00:09:09.348 --> 00:09:12.251
and being really adept at
working at the interface

00:09:12.251 --> 00:09:14.954
between industry
and the government.

00:09:16.122 --> 00:09:19.058 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's an area that's
really hard to navigate.

00:09:19.058 --> 00:09:22.595 align:start size:94% position:6%
Not very many people
have that whole skillset.

00:09:22.595 --> 00:09:25.064
Maybe very, very few that,

00:09:25.064 --> 00:09:28.167 align:start position:0%
that understand regulatory
affairs and clinical development

00:09:28.167 --> 00:09:32.905
and all the other stuff,
and can talk govy talk,

00:09:32.905 --> 00:09:35.575
but also can talk to
the private sector

00:09:35.575 --> 00:09:37.209
and work with them.

00:09:37.209 --> 00:09:38.878
So that was kind of my thing.

00:09:38.878 --> 00:09:42.682 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I've been involved
in many outbreaks,

00:09:42.682 --> 00:09:44.250
was right at the
tip of the spear

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:46.419
in drug repurposing for Zika.

00:09:48.955 --> 00:09:52.892
Was really asked by
Department of Defense

00:09:52.892 --> 00:09:55.094
and Defense Threat
Reduction Agency

00:09:55.094 --> 00:09:58.064
to step in and solve a hot mess.

00:09:58.064 --> 00:10:01.801
That was a small
mid-western company

00:10:01.801 --> 00:10:03.803
that owned the rights to a-

00:10:03.803 --> 00:10:06.305
I'm sorry, a Ebola
vaccine candidate

00:10:06.305 --> 00:10:09.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they'd bought from
Canada for about $150,000.

00:10:11.077 --> 00:10:12.678
And I carried that forward.

00:10:12.678 --> 00:10:15.314 align:start size:84% position:16%
That was a, you
know, amazing time.

00:10:15.314 --> 00:10:17.583
People were scared
outta their brains.

00:10:17.583 --> 00:10:19.418
Ebola's a scary business.

00:10:19.418 --> 00:10:22.388
Way more scary than
than the Corona.

00:10:24.724 --> 00:10:28.628
And it drives people
crazy and, you know,

00:10:28.628 --> 00:10:32.598 align:start size:97% position:3%
they'll shoot each other
and ebola's a scary disease.

00:10:32.598 --> 00:10:36.135 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so I got Merck
involved in that project

00:10:36.135 --> 00:10:37.570
and got them to buy the product

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:39.038 align:start size:84% position:16%
from the small
midwestern company,

00:10:39.038 --> 00:10:41.774
and that's now the
Merck Ebola vaccine.

00:10:41.774 --> 00:10:43.743
So I've been doing
this a long time.

00:10:43.743 --> 00:10:47.813 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then, then, and when
you're in this space,

00:10:47.813 --> 00:10:51.651 align:start size:97% position:3%
you touch on the intelligence
community all the time.

00:10:52.952 --> 00:10:57.657 align:start size:88% position:13%
They are all thick
throughout bio-defense.

00:10:57.657 --> 00:11:00.259 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so I've had many
contacts that have been CIA.

00:11:00.259 --> 00:11:01.427
I'm not CIA.

00:11:01.427 --> 00:11:03.362
I'm not trained CIA.

00:11:03.362 --> 00:11:06.232
I have been security cleared
by the Department of Defense.

00:11:06.232 --> 00:11:09.902
I'm sorry, Stu Peters,
you're wrong on that.

00:11:09.902 --> 00:11:11.404
And George Webb,
but there it is.

00:11:11.404 --> 00:11:12.838
- Yeah.

00:11:12.838 --> 00:11:17.276 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, and so if you're
in this business area,

00:11:17.276 --> 00:11:20.046
you touch on CIA
folk all the time.

00:11:20.046 --> 00:11:23.683 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I've actually co-published
with one that is now a

00:11:23.683 --> 00:11:28.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
retired CIA officer who
arguably is our top expert

00:11:29.555 --> 00:11:32.224
in gain of function research.

00:11:32.224 --> 00:11:35.494
A former project
manager at DARPA

00:11:35.494 --> 00:11:37.296
named Dr. Michael Callahan,

00:11:37.296 --> 00:11:39.632 align:start size:84% position:16%
who has an appointment
at Harvard.

00:11:42.435 --> 00:11:46.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he called me up on January
2nd, 2020 or January 4th,

00:11:46.205 --> 00:11:47.339
right around there and said,

00:11:47.339 --> 00:11:49.075
Hey Robert, we got a problem.

00:11:49.075 --> 00:11:54.013
This novel Coronavirus that's
circulating here in Wuhan,

00:11:54.013 --> 00:11:56.082 align:start size:84% position:16%
looks like it's
gonna be a problem.

00:11:56.082 --> 00:11:57.983
And that's what got me going.

00:11:58.951 --> 00:12:00.219
And that's kind of where the,

00:12:00.219 --> 00:12:02.755 align:start size:88% position:13%
the journey of the last
three years begins.

00:12:02.755 --> 00:12:06.592 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I did a threat
assessment, as I always do.

00:12:06.592 --> 00:12:08.127
He asked me to get my,

00:12:08.127 --> 00:12:11.130
the group that I work
with kind of wound up

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:13.799
and ready to respond to this.

00:12:13.799 --> 00:12:15.234
And I,

00:12:15.234 --> 00:12:18.304 align:start size:97% position:3%
I did the usual threat
assessment and my sense was

00:12:18.304 --> 00:12:21.507
that we could not build a
safe and effective vaccine

