00:36:39.363 --> 00:36:40.898 align:start size:84% position:16%
Department of Defense
in particular,

00:36:40.898 --> 00:36:43.601 align:start size:88% position:13%
but most clients really
don't like to have

00:36:44.835 --> 00:36:48.239
the consultant be out front.

00:36:50.708 --> 00:36:54.345 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, so you have the
initial issue is the coercion,

00:36:54.345 --> 00:36:56.280
then the vaccines are out.

00:36:56.280 --> 00:37:00.184 align:start size:88% position:13%
You already had heard
of some vaccine injuries

00:37:00.184 --> 00:37:01.686
and things of that nature.

00:37:01.686 --> 00:37:03.454
But when did it shift, yeah?
- And then this other Canadian

00:37:03.454 --> 00:37:05.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
comes in, Byron Brighton.
- Yeah.

00:37:06.324 --> 00:37:09.227 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So Byron, who I'd
never heard of before,

00:37:09.227 --> 00:37:12.563 align:start size:97% position:3%
but he's an absolutely well
qualified vaccinologist

00:37:12.563 --> 00:37:16.167
immunologist academic.

00:37:16.167 --> 00:37:18.436
University of Gelf.

00:37:18.436 --> 00:37:19.270
Is that right?

00:37:19.270 --> 00:37:20.938
I think.

00:37:20.938 --> 00:37:23.507
I may be confusing him
with Mattias Desmet.

00:37:23.507 --> 00:37:24.875
So forgive me if I did.

00:37:27.578 --> 00:37:30.948 align:start size:84% position:16%
So Byron apparently
manages to grab

00:37:32.750 --> 00:37:36.420
the common technical
document for the Pfizer

00:37:39.557 --> 00:37:43.761 align:start size:88% position:13%
submission for allowance
to proceed in humans.

00:37:44.996 --> 00:37:47.098
So common technical document,

00:37:47.098 --> 00:37:49.267
we used to call those INDs.

00:37:49.267 --> 00:37:52.803 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's the dossier of everything
they know about the product

00:37:54.672 --> 00:37:57.808
and that they used to justify
initiating clinical trials.

00:38:01.112 --> 00:38:03.547
And, and usually those are
considered confidential.

00:38:03.547 --> 00:38:06.384 align:start size:88% position:13%
So the likes of you
and I can't get at them

00:38:06.384 --> 00:38:08.519
because otherwise we
would be able to go

00:38:08.519 --> 00:38:12.657
and try to reverse engineer
whatever the new product is.

00:38:12.657 --> 00:38:14.892
And so for whatever reason,

00:38:14.892 --> 00:38:18.095 align:start size:88% position:13%
the Japanese apparently
put this, you know.

00:38:18.095 --> 00:38:20.965
Common technical document is
basically universal document

00:38:20.965 --> 00:38:23.601
that gets submitted to
all regulatory agencies

00:38:23.601 --> 00:38:25.002
all over the world.

00:38:25.002 --> 00:38:26.504
And they'd put it on a server

00:38:26.504 --> 00:38:28.673 align:start size:84% position:16%
and Byron had
somehow grabbed it,

00:38:28.673 --> 00:38:31.609
or one of his buddies
had and sent it to him

00:38:31.609 --> 00:38:33.611
and he'd done an analysis of it.

00:38:35.146 --> 00:38:36.814
Most of it's in Japanese,

00:38:36.814 --> 00:38:40.551 align:start size:88% position:13%
but the figures and
legends are in English.

00:38:40.551 --> 00:38:41.952
And so he went through that

00:38:41.952 --> 00:38:44.689 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he was pretty
shocked by what he found,

00:38:44.689 --> 00:38:47.391 align:start size:94% position:6%
but he's not a
regulatory affairs expert

00:38:47.391 --> 00:38:49.593 align:start size:84% position:16%
and he doesn't have
the kind of, you know,

00:38:49.593 --> 00:38:51.962
hybrid experience I have.

00:38:51.962 --> 00:38:55.399
So he asked a buddy of mine
that runs the company called

00:38:55.399 --> 00:38:56.701
Trial Site News

00:38:57.902 --> 00:39:00.838
to see whether or not I
would take a look at it.

00:39:02.340 --> 00:39:05.609
So it comes to me right
after it goes to Byron

00:39:05.609 --> 00:39:07.845
and I'm asked to do an
independent assessment

00:39:09.313 --> 00:39:14.051
and I read through the thing
and I'm pretty shocked and,

00:39:14.785 --> 00:39:17.855
at how shoddy it is.

00:39:17.855 --> 00:39:19.924
It's just, it's atrocious

00:39:22.326 --> 00:39:24.795
and shocking to me

00:39:24.795 --> 00:39:27.865 align:start size:88% position:13%
that this would be
submitted for anything.

00:39:27.865 --> 00:39:29.266
I mean, if I submitted that,

00:39:30.434 --> 00:39:31.936
I would expect to
get reprimanded,

00:39:31.936 --> 00:39:36.040
if not basically tarred
and feathered by the FDA.

00:39:36.040 --> 00:39:37.441
- Was that basic,

00:39:37.441 --> 00:39:39.110 align:start position:0%
was that because of the efficacy
of what you were seeing?

00:39:39.110 --> 00:39:40.378
What, what was the most shocking

00:39:40.378 --> 00:39:41.645 align:start size:81% position:19%
part of it?
- No, it was just,

00:39:41.645 --> 00:39:44.482
there was no real efficacy data.

00:39:44.482 --> 00:39:47.051
They weren't even testing
the real drug product.

00:39:47.051 --> 00:39:49.186 align:start size:84% position:16%
They were substituting
an mRNA coating

00:39:49.186 --> 00:39:51.756 align:start size:84% position:16%
for a firefly
protein luciferase.

00:39:53.257 --> 00:39:56.394 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So it was the methodology
of the whole thing, basically?

00:39:57.728 --> 00:40:01.565 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It was completely
inadequate by any standards

00:40:01.565 --> 00:40:03.834
that I had been trained on.

00:40:03.834 --> 00:40:06.070 align:start size:84% position:16%
Completely inadequate
as a package.

00:40:06.070 --> 00:40:07.905
And so I wrote up my assessment

00:40:07.905 --> 00:40:12.276 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I was so concerned by what
I thought I saw, you know.

00:40:12.276 --> 00:40:14.311 align:start size:84% position:16%
This can't possibly
be coming from Pfizer,

00:40:14.311 --> 00:40:17.415 align:start size:84% position:16%
this can't possibly
be, you know,

00:40:17.415 --> 00:40:18.916
I must be missing something.

00:40:18.916 --> 00:40:21.786 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I sent it to a more
senior regulatory affairs guy

00:40:21.786 --> 00:40:25.055
that I know that I
used to work with

00:40:25.055 --> 00:40:27.992 align:start size:84% position:16%
at another contract
research organization.

00:40:27.992 --> 00:40:29.660
And he looked it
over and he said,

00:40:29.660 --> 00:40:32.229 align:start size:97% position:3%
oh yeah, Robert, everything
you said here is true,

00:40:32.229 --> 00:40:33.898
but you missed a
couple of other.

00:40:35.699 --> 00:40:38.269
And so I said, well,
okay, that's great.

00:40:38.269 --> 00:40:42.273
I need to write this up now as
an essay for Trial Site News.

00:40:42.273 --> 00:40:43.240
Do you want your name on it?

00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:44.608
He's like, no, no, no, no, no!

00:40:46.076 --> 00:40:48.979
Don't put my name anywhere,
anywhere near this thing!

00:40:48.979 --> 00:40:53.751
So I boldly went where
angels fear to tread

00:40:53.751 --> 00:40:55.486
and put that thing out.

00:40:57.555 --> 00:41:00.424 align:start size:84% position:16%
And Byron in
parallel, put his out.

00:41:00.424 --> 00:41:03.694
And I've, I've since met and,
and spent time with Byron,

00:41:03.694 --> 00:41:08.699 align:start size:97% position:3%
he tells a story up in Canada
that he was scared silly

00:41:09.433 --> 00:41:10.935
of what I was gonna say.

00:41:10.935 --> 00:41:13.471 align:start size:94% position:6%
He thought I was this big,
intimidating, nasty guy

00:41:13.471 --> 00:41:14.839 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I was gonna
tear him to shreds.

00:41:14.839 --> 00:41:16.974 align:start size:84% position:16%
And when I basically
piped up on the,

00:41:16.974 --> 00:41:20.377
on the zoom call that we
had over this and said,

00:41:20.377 --> 00:41:21.946 align:start size:88% position:13%
Byron, I agree with
everything you've said,

00:41:21.946 --> 00:41:23.747
plus there's these other things.

00:41:23.747 --> 00:41:25.716
He was completely relieved.

00:41:25.716 --> 00:41:28.819 align:start size:84% position:16%
But that's, that's how
that came to be is,

00:41:28.819 --> 00:41:32.022
is that's how I ended up
being one of the first,

00:41:32.022 --> 00:41:35.659
if not the first, to
me and Byron at least,

00:41:35.659 --> 00:41:39.630
to really break the story
that the regulatory package

00:41:39.630 --> 00:41:42.900
that Pfizer had submitted
was grossly inadequate.

00:41:42.900 --> 00:41:46.270
That set off a cascade of
me talking to Peter Marks.

00:41:46.270 --> 00:41:48.072
And you know, I, at that point,

00:41:48.072 --> 00:41:53.043
I was still assuming that the
FDA was acting in good faith.

00:41:54.378 --> 00:41:56.747
And my core assumption was that

00:41:56.747 --> 00:42:00.084
Pfizer had pulled a
fast one on the FDA.

00:42:00.084 --> 00:42:03.287 align:start position:0%
That the FDA didn't have
sufficient scientific expertise

00:42:03.287 --> 00:42:04.755
to understand what
they were looking at.

00:42:04.755 --> 00:42:06.357
They couldn't possibly!

00:42:06.357 --> 00:42:08.759 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's no way this could
get through the FDA.

00:42:08.759 --> 00:42:11.061 align:start size:94% position:6%
The only way I could
imagine would be if they,

00:42:11.061 --> 00:42:14.064 align:start size:88% position:13%
if the people that were
doing the reviewing

00:42:14.064 --> 00:42:16.500 align:start size:94% position:6%
just didn't understand
what they were looking at.

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:19.103
And so I offered my help
to Peter Marks and said,

00:42:19.103 --> 00:42:21.272 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, I understand
all this tech.

00:42:21.272 --> 00:42:26.110 align:start size:88% position:13%
And he basically told
me to go pound sand and,

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:30.648
and that another body of data
had been submitted by Pfizer.

00:42:30.648 --> 00:42:32.349
And he'd reviewed that

00:42:32.349 --> 00:42:37.221
and he actually asked me to
be quiet about my concerns,

00:42:37.221 --> 00:42:39.323
to give him time to disclose

00:42:39.323 --> 00:42:41.725
what was in this
new Pfizer dossier.

00:42:43.327 --> 00:42:45.763
Which we now know
the FDA and Pfizer

00:42:45.763 --> 00:42:48.432
tried to withhold from
the public for 75 years,

00:42:48.432 --> 00:42:50.200
until the courts forced them to.

00:42:50.200 --> 00:42:52.870
And so basically I got
lied to by Peter Marks,

00:42:52.870 --> 00:42:57.374 align:start size:94% position:6%
but that now in retrospect
shouldn't surprise me.

00:42:57.374 --> 00:42:58.909 align:start size:88% position:13%
- What's so
fascinating about this,

00:42:58.909 --> 00:43:01.211
beyond the science that's
obviously fascinating,

00:43:01.211 --> 00:43:03.681
is that you're sort of
laying out the story

00:43:03.681 --> 00:43:05.082
of your red pilling in essence

00:43:05.082 --> 00:43:06.884
because every step
of the way here,

00:43:06.884 --> 00:43:08.285 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's some
regulatory commission

00:43:08.285 --> 00:43:11.622 align:start size:94% position:6%
or some sort of
censorious outlet that's,

00:43:11.622 --> 00:43:12.856
that's kind of stopping you

00:43:12.856 --> 00:43:14.592
or that even there
you still are saying

00:43:14.592 --> 00:43:17.227
you had this belief that
the FDA would stop it.

00:43:17.227 --> 00:43:18.696
So when did,

00:43:18.696 --> 00:43:19.930
when did you realize that
it sort of seemed like

00:43:19.930 --> 00:43:22.666 align:start size:84% position:16%
the entire machinery
was either broken

00:43:22.666 --> 00:43:25.736
or my words just
outright negligent?

00:43:29.106 --> 00:43:30.941
- That's a, that's
a good question.

00:43:34.945 --> 00:43:39.316
As the adverse events piled up,

00:43:39.316 --> 00:43:40.584
and, and I,

00:43:40.584 --> 00:43:41.852
I'm vaccine injured.

00:43:43.554 --> 00:43:45.456
I took the jab,

00:43:45.456 --> 00:43:49.093
I took Moderna when
the National Guard

00:43:50.794 --> 00:43:53.297
was deploying it here
in Central Virginia.

00:43:54.431 --> 00:43:56.834
I was scheduled for
a talk in France

00:43:56.834 --> 00:43:59.937
and I knew I couldn't go
if I didn't take the jab.

00:44:01.305 --> 00:44:03.407 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I had long COVID and
there was a whole lot of press

00:44:03.407 --> 00:44:07.011
that long COVID could be, could
benefit from taking the jab.

00:44:07.011 --> 00:44:10.581 align:start size:97% position:3%
We now know that's a lie, but,
you know, more propaganda.

00:44:10.581 --> 00:44:11.949
- How skeptical were you?

00:44:11.949 --> 00:44:14.051
I mean, knowing that
you had had COVID.

00:44:14.051 --> 00:44:15.119
Okay, you had long COVID,

00:44:15.119 --> 00:44:16.053
but you had done research.

00:44:16.053 --> 00:44:17.287
I mean, how skeptical were you?

00:44:17.287 --> 00:44:18.522
I get you wanna

00:44:18.522 --> 00:44:19.890 align:start size:84% position:16%
go to France, but
- About the jab, so.

00:44:19.890 --> 00:44:22.359
So I, I had, number one,

00:44:24.161 --> 00:44:25.829
in good faith,

00:44:25.829 --> 00:44:29.500
I'd assumed that my colleagues
that I had known for decades

00:44:30.434 --> 00:44:31.935
must have had a.

00:44:33.303 --> 00:44:35.105 align:start size:94% position:6%
Another part of the story
that we didn't cover

00:44:35.105 --> 00:44:38.108
is that Jill and I
worked on this tech

00:44:38.108 --> 00:44:40.110
for over a decade in academia.

00:44:41.879 --> 00:44:44.715
At California Regional
Private Research Center

00:44:44.715 --> 00:44:47.418
at Bounces Labs, at U Maryland.

00:44:48.419 --> 00:44:49.687
And we abandoned it

00:44:49.687 --> 00:44:51.889
because we could never
overcome the toxicity.

00:44:53.791 --> 00:44:55.893 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we have multiple
other patents.

00:44:55.893 --> 00:44:58.295 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, this wall behind
me is all patents,

00:44:59.763 --> 00:45:02.933
multiple other patents for
different cationic lipids,

00:45:02.933 --> 00:45:04.768
different formulations.

00:45:04.768 --> 00:45:07.171 align:start size:97% position:3%
We published the first
stuff on use of cholesterol,

00:45:07.171 --> 00:45:08.872
which is in these formulations.

00:45:10.207 --> 00:45:11.909
And early on I called up

00:45:13.277 --> 00:45:17.281 align:start size:97% position:3%
a guy at University of British
Columbia, Peter Culles,

00:45:18.849 --> 00:45:20.818
who I'd known for quite a while,

00:45:20.818 --> 00:45:22.820 align:start size:84% position:16%
who was the one
that had really made

00:45:22.820 --> 00:45:25.656
the breakthroughs
to enable this,

00:45:25.656 --> 00:45:27.825
this version that
was being deployed.

00:45:29.293 --> 00:45:33.297
And I had faith that they
had solved those problems

00:45:33.297 --> 00:45:35.899 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we had not
been able to solve.

00:45:35.899 --> 00:45:36.734
- [Dave] Hmm.

00:45:38.469 --> 00:45:42.072 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So I was still
operating assuming that

00:45:43.474 --> 00:45:45.909
they had overcome
the things that had

00:45:45.909 --> 00:45:49.379
bedeviled us for a decade.

00:45:49.379 --> 00:45:52.082
- That's a heck of a
leap of faith, huh?

00:45:52.082 --> 00:45:53.317
- Well, I think it's reasonable.

00:45:53.317 --> 00:45:54.518
I mean, you,

00:45:54.518 --> 00:45:57.855 align:start size:88% position:13%
you can assume that
people are are nefarious

00:45:57.855 --> 00:46:01.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
or you can assume that
people are generally good.

00:46:02.593 --> 00:46:05.662
And I prefer to assume that
people are generally good.

00:46:05.662 --> 00:46:07.765
Although I gotta say,
the last three years

00:46:07.765 --> 00:46:09.566
that's taken a pretty hard hit.

00:46:10.400 --> 00:46:11.401
- Yeah.

00:46:11.401 --> 00:46:12.836
- But I still,

00:46:12.836 --> 00:46:14.438 align:start size:94% position:6%
I still approach the
world with an open heart.

00:46:14.438 --> 00:46:15.606
I try hard to.

00:46:16.740 --> 00:46:17.975
- Yeah.

00:46:17.975 --> 00:46:22.012
- And I try hard to have
faith in my fellow man

00:46:22.012 --> 00:46:27.017 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I employ the three strikes
rule, generally speaking.

00:46:28.218 --> 00:46:30.454
You know, takes three
strikes for me to say,

00:46:30.454 --> 00:46:32.790
I just don't wanna deal
with that person again.

00:46:37.961 --> 00:46:42.199
I extended trust to
my former colleagues

00:46:42.199 --> 00:46:44.468 align:start size:81% position:19%
believing that
they must have had

00:46:44.468 --> 00:46:47.171
the breakthrough that they
asserted that they had had.

00:46:48.906 --> 00:46:52.543
And obviously in retrospect,

00:46:53.710 --> 00:46:56.814
they had convinced
themselves as a,

00:46:56.814 --> 00:47:00.584
as a community of
a number of things,

00:47:00.584 --> 00:47:04.221 align:start size:97% position:3%
such as minor modifications
in the structure

00:47:04.221 --> 00:47:06.490
of the positively charged fat

00:47:06.490 --> 00:47:09.459
would lead to formulations,

00:47:09.459 --> 00:47:12.663
which would target specific
organ systems or tissues.

00:47:13.864 --> 00:47:15.232
And so they, they
had published and,

00:47:15.232 --> 00:47:17.668
and it was believed
in the field that

00:47:17.668 --> 00:47:20.237
these modified versions.

00:47:21.438 --> 00:47:23.674
In my lab, we'd built
hundreds of different

00:47:23.674 --> 00:47:27.311
cationic lipid compounds
and tested them at Genzyme.

00:47:27.311 --> 00:47:30.247
They tested thousands
or tens of thousands

00:47:31.348 --> 00:47:32.583 align:start size:84% position:16%
when they were
trying to get agents

00:47:32.583 --> 00:47:34.952
to treat cystic fibrosis.

00:47:34.952 --> 00:47:37.921
So I knew how the
game was played and

00:47:37.921 --> 00:47:42.893 align:start size:97% position:3%
the literature suggested that
there'd been some kind of

00:47:42.893 --> 00:47:45.662
serendipitous magic findings

00:47:45.662 --> 00:47:48.932 align:start size:97% position:3%
that if you use this
structure and you injected it,

00:47:48.932 --> 00:47:51.902
it would only go to the lymph
nodes and it would stay there.

00:47:51.902 --> 00:47:54.304
And you'll recall, that's
what we were all told.

00:47:55.339 --> 00:47:56.240
And yet,

00:47:58.008 --> 00:47:59.476
that wasn't true.

00:47:59.476 --> 00:48:02.346
It was clearly not true based
on the Pfizer data package

00:48:02.346 --> 00:48:03.914
that Byron Bridal had obtained.

00:48:05.382 --> 00:48:09.386
So, you know, when you're,
when you're in, you know,

00:48:09.386 --> 00:48:11.922 align:start size:94% position:6%
you spent your whole
career working in an area,

00:48:13.523 --> 00:48:16.526 align:start size:94% position:6%
you have all these
relationships with people,

00:48:16.526 --> 00:48:18.428
professional relationships.

00:48:19.796 --> 00:48:22.633
To break out of that and,

00:48:22.633 --> 00:48:24.468
and say, oh, you know,

00:48:24.468 --> 00:48:27.237
all these things I thought
were this way, were wrong.

00:48:27.237 --> 00:48:28.705
And for me,

00:48:28.705 --> 00:48:31.275 align:start size:97% position:3%
so I not only had that on
kind of a micro level in this

00:48:31.275 --> 00:48:33.577
little bubble of
this technology,

00:48:33.577 --> 00:48:35.312
but I think we've all had that,

00:48:35.312 --> 00:48:38.682
those of us that are awake
have had that same experience

00:48:38.682 --> 00:48:43.687 align:start size:97% position:3%
about our government, media,
military, all, you know,

00:48:46.623 --> 00:48:49.760
globalism, you know.

00:48:49.760 --> 00:48:52.129
